{
	title: 'Reliable iOS composer keyboard and viewport recovery',
	spec: 'In the iOS browser and installed web app, tapping the enabled composer opens the software keyboard, including after sending a prompt, dismissing the keyboard, switching conversation tabs, opening and closing native dialogs, rotation, and resuming the app. When the keyboard is absent the app occupies the available visual viewport; it must not remain at a keyboard-reduced height with unused space underneath. Programmatic focus must not leave the composer in a state where a direct tap cannot open the keyboard. Preserve native caret selection, accessibility, draft durability, and the single transcript scroller. Verify these transitions on an actual iOS device; Chromium emulation alone is not evidence that WebKit keyboard behavior works. Establish the failing transition before choosing a workaround; do not add polling or force blur/refocus on every tap.',
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'Investigation: the reported failure still allowed transcript scrolling, while tapping the empty composer did not show the keyboard. The screenshot showed unused space below a keyboard-height app. No root cause or failing transition is confirmed: native scrolling can continue on the compositor even if JavaScript is blocked. Composer is disabled only by an open question, not by processing state; its placeholder has pointer-events: none. viewport.sync writes visualViewport geometry on resize and scroll, with no resume resynchronization. Targeted Chromium checks of completion taps, login/streaming/input, and status-dialog interaction passed (3 tests, 58 assertions); these exercise the working tree, not the affected iOS page. Host diagnostics contain no browser-side focus or runtime-error telemetry, so they cannot establish what happened in the page. Keep this task planned until reproduced and verified on iOS.',
		'Evidence (state/web-diag.log, Home Screen app, 393x793): visualViewport.height stayed 485 (offsetTop 0) while innerHeight was 793 for 8+ minutes across resumes, and even from page load (a fresh page reported 485 at start); it entered the state while hidden (458 reported while backgrounded) and recovered only after a later keyboard open/close. The app sized from visualViewport, leaving the unused band under it. Genuine keyboard states in this app look different: innerHeight shrinks with it (390/390 offset 403, 417/417 offset 376). Fix: viewport.page() uses the layout viewport (innerWidth/innerHeight, offset 0) whenever no text field has focus and scale is 1, since no keyboard can be open then, and re-reads on focusin/focusout, window resize, pageshow and visibilitychange (a stale viewport fires no event). Not polling; no blur/refocus. Verified only in headless Chromium at 393x793 with touch emulation and visualViewport.height overridden to 485: app 793 at load, follows the viewport (485, then 400) while the composer is focused, back to 793 on blur and on visibilitychange. Not verified on iOS. Still unexplained: 538/417 offset 254 persisted ~20 s while the composer was tapped three times (the tap-without-keyboard symptom); whether the composer held focus then is unknown, now captured by composerFocused/appHeight in diagnostics. Keep planned until verified on a device.'
	],
	needs: ['1b']
}
